 like gojo anonymity is  a clear revolutionary in the making he  has an extreme distaste for the current  global system which he expressed in a  more social manner there was a very cool  flashback showcasing how empty his life  was managing money of the rich and how  he saw humble bakeries as far more  important to society at large but even  without the flashback we had a clear  picture of him pretty much immediately  after he said well jiu-jitsu sorcerers  they're idiots but working in an office  is idiotic so i chose the lesser of two  evils which is a great example of what i  mean by efficient that really did tell  me everything that his flashback ended  up conveying just with no emotion  attached to it which isn't to say that  the flashback was unnecessary it  definitely wasn't because that emotion  is very very important but it's more of  a credit to akhitami for flawlessly  conveying who this man is without  needing a flashback to do so the  flashback was just the icing on the cake  in delicious  none of me cake and on top of that he  just had so many badass lines throughout  the sock my favorite of which was the  office worker style thread of  it's 5 30. i started work at 10 today so  no matter what  i'm clocking out by six never before has  office language sounded so  wonderfully threatening and i also love  that his office lifestyle has carried  over so strongly into his sorcery as  well i found the overtime condition  particularly interesting because i love  the idea of limiting your powers during  standard work hours in order to  significantly increase your curse  prowess  in the event that one is forced to work  overtime and i also believe he had  another restriction which was to reveal  exactly how his curse technique worked  which  i don't know if this was literally  stated i forget to be honest with you  but surely this once again results in  some sort of power boost for basically  kneecapping himself by giving the  enemies that information  it reminds me a lot of gentry from  hunter hunter who had a very similar  condition where he needed to disclose  exactly how his non-abilities operated  as an activation requirement there's  just something i really love about power  systems where yeah sure you can achieve  high output but you also need to pay an  equal price for that power and the price  that you dear viewer may need to pay is  that of subscribing to the new world  review  which will set a binding vowel granting  regular injections of jujutsukaisen  content administered straight into your  youtube feed a very powerful and highly  advantageous mechanic that simply should  not be ignored but speaking of curse  mechanics and structure get some very  nice expansions here as well during the  arc something that really took my eye is  the idea of the fly heads actually  mostly because i'm glad that we're not  just you know outright neglecting  lower level curses the whole idea of the  great hierarchy kind of spooked me  because i feared that it would be  immediately made  irrelevant but it's nice to see that  there are some genuine and interesting  uses at the very least for these lower  grade existences  i mean putting them to work to find out  if someone is a curse user or not that's  a cool little utility idea that keeps  the lower grades somewhat relevant and i  also quite  bizarrely enjoyed the additional idea of  the sorcerer versus curse grade system  being staggered like it's not as simple  as grade 1 sorcery is equal to grade 1  curse and it makes all the sense in the  world because if your job was to  exterminate these things then why would  you give them an even match up so yes a  grade 2 sorcerer should absolutely wipe  the floor with a grade 2 curse which in  the end isn't all that big of a deal but  it gives the series that tiny bit more  depth the idea that there is this  thoroughly thought out human bureaucracy  at play and the idea that not everything  can be summed up by some nice  neat little system very reminiscent of  real life meanwhile the concept of  residuals is also quite fun because once  again it adds an element of risk to  using cursed energy but along with that  it also adds strategy say if you wanted  to deliberately leave a residual trail  fun stuff to play with as an author i  imagine if you can keep track of it all  but now we're going to move to one hell  of a  crazy mofo being mahito this arc delves  so much more into him than i really  expected things to this early on but he  holds a highly dangerous yet very  interesting philosophy the concept of  life containing no weight  or value and in his mind there is no  limitation on how life  should or could be used for personal  benefit he's very much the anti-yuji who  began the series with the idea that all  life holds value  even those deemed worthless by society  and we'll get to eugene in a bit because  uh yeah but mahito holds a very  appropriate technique for his views  being that he's able to manipulate souls  also  very casual confirmation of the  existence of souls there one of the most  complex discussions in all of humanity  and we've just solved it right here but  what nahito represents is very much  counter to everything that i suppose we  are theoretically  supposed to believe in his values which  does make some sense as he is a cursed  spirit himself born of hatred i believe  what's really interesting though is that  as much as he tells junepei not to be  indifferent mahito himself seems quite  indifferent towards humanity right now  like he definitely doesn't see the value  in life but he also hasn't been shown as  wildly driven to get rid of it either  right now he's just screwing around with  his grotesque experiments having a bit  of fun  and i'm sure that there is a much deeper  drive somewhere because for a creature  born specifically of hatred there's just  got to be something stronger working  which is definitely hinted at because  mahito is pretty directly equated to  gojo  having this sense of superficiality but  also a deep dark well of power which is  also terrifying because he is still  developing  said power i loved his domain expansion  though there was a true sense of dread  when mojito activated  it and i felt for sure that nanome was  going to die especially because that was  the very moment where his flashback was  shown and usually seeing a character's  history  is a pretty good sign that they're about  to die imminently but the sukuna twist  there was a very nice way of dealing  with all of that but now we need to talk  about yuji because he faced  and arguably is still facing quite a  crisis earlier i mentioned that he sees  value in all life but he really did have  to confront that guiding philosophy  right here  and in an intriguing twist for a shona  manga yuji did not quite manage to  overcome it  arguably he didn't overcome it at all  even at the very end of the battle yuji  was hell  bent on killing mahito killing yuji  gasp especially since majito is a cursed  spirit you could say exercising him but  no yuji wanted to  kill him yuji's beliefs failed him and  after witnessing what happened to jinpei  yuji is  is kind of broken and he did kill during  this arc make no mistake there was no  clean way for yuji to get out of this he  is stained from here on out and at this  point all he can really do is reevaluate  where he stands in life or even how he  views life it was a pretty deep and  distressing arc for yuji  and yet another in a growing line of  events where he was pretty much  powerless to save people in the way he  wanted and there's something of a  morbidly charming quality about watching  yuji fail because all of these  experiences are going to add up to  either one glorious climax where he  finally does save someone or  alternatively  in one big moment of self-implosion and  i really do enjoy  not knowing what path we'll end up with  i'm not 100 that this story ends well  for him and if it does i'm not confident  that it will do so without a lot more  pain and suffering pushing him beyond  his breaking point eventually but now to  jinpei and man i feel a little bit sick  just saying his name because  what a tragedy watching his story here  was like seeing a train wreck in slow  motion from all the stuff about his high  school to the really twisted  mentor-mentee relationship that he  formed with majito and ultimately being  pushed over the edge then brought back  briefly by yuji  only to have his life snuffed out for  good and as part of this i also want to  underscore his mother i knew the very  moment she showed up that she was  probably as good as dead  you need to be very careful about  lovable tertiary characters in a series  like this  because chances are they are  deliberately designed to be as likable  as possible within the shortest amount  of time  simply so that you can feel the pain of  their loss and there was also something  very interesting about the way in which  her death was communicated  it wasn't seen on screen it wasn't  stated by another character through  dialogue it wasn't even given by a  narrator it was just reported  in pure prose at the beginning of a  chapter which may sound simple and even  underwhelming but that is very unique  for the manga medium and it gave  jiujitsukai scent a brief moment of this  novelistic magic where peak imagination  was activated especially with the  horrific details of what happened to her  body and that it was found stuffed in a  bed with ice packs like really  excruciatingly detailed stuff that has  no place being conveyed visually i don't  think there's anything akatami could  have drawn that would have been more  powerful than just reading these words  and it's very much making me appreciate  the keen intuition that akatami has for  situations like this because he is  clearly not  afraid to depict brutality but in this  case he knew when to hold back  and how this event would have peak  impact but all in all like i said it's  truly tragic  perfectly set up as well with plenty of  moments of hope one of which was when  yuji and jinpei were bonding over movies  which was also a nice callback to the  previous arc but the whole setup gave me  the very distinct impression that yeah  jinpei's mother she was probably done  for but he might have a chance  jinpe himself could be brought back from  the brink and become a new student at  the jujutsu high school the setup for  that was definitely there but alchemy  took all of that and said  no this is going to be one of those  times when something horrible just  happens there is no last minute  salvation no resurrection or at least  i hope not i guess i don't know for sure  but the general idea is that you're just  going to be served a dish of raw  tragedy so bon appetit and as sad as it  is i do appreciate that because  personally i'm a little scarred and i  feel like i've lost a character with  jinpe and that helps me identify a lot  more with yuji because that would be  exactly what he's feeling although in  his case it would be on a significantly  more extreme level so we had quite a  somber ending to the ark although i  should also note there was plenty of  comedy to be had as well it was quite  well balanced especially with stuff like  yuji stealing the teachers pants but  then even that that teacher gets turned  around on us and hits the audience with  some hard drama right at the end of the  arc on something i haven't mentioned yet  it was also great to see yuji developing  his skills i really like the  introduction of how his cursed energy  works with the whole not being able to  keep up with his superhuman speed  resulting in this bizarre kind of double  strike style effect there's such a nice  simplicity about it but it's also not so  simple that it equates to  hmm i hit you with strong fist now it's  not that there's a bit of nuance to it  which is greatly appreciated and really  the only bad thing to say about t